    {
      "id": "bkHC",
      "code_hash": "d6ce4536728aaae933d242f8e8bbffe3",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"1-what-compounds-do-i-actually-have\">1. What compounds do I actually have?</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>Where B comes from:</strong> stored CATMoS predictions in the model authors'\n<a href=\"https://github.com/kmansouri/OPERA/blob/01b07c769dd86eadb71843e057783a64a364c885/OPERA_Data.zip\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">OPERA data archive</a>,\nspecifically <code>CATMoS_QR50k.sdf</code>, field <code>CATMoS_LD50_pred</code>. The archive is pinned\nto commit <code>01b07c7</code>; structures, domain flags and training tags remain in the\nlocal B release. I did not generate these estimates by running OPERA locally.\nThe <strong>173-structure EPA TEST collection is separate</strong> and is not the target\nbehind the B classifier. A below uses measured experimental outcomes.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Stored TEST coverage is now <strong>173 full structures</strong>; those stored estimates\nremain separate from experiments. My original prepared selection contains <strong>60 selected herbicides and 116\nselected natural products</strong>. Those are selection labels. For a stricter\nbiological comparison, the reviewed set contains <strong>19 natural-occurrence\ncompounds: 5 active under K1998 and 14 activity-unknown</strong>, with <strong>0 reviewed\nnatural low-response examples under that policy</strong> and 58 distinct herbicide\ncomparators. I cannot call unknown compounds non-herbicidal.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Below I join that reviewed grouping to each new toxicity cohort by the full\nstructure key. Counts are unique compounds, not dose rows or studies. A\ncommercial comparator label does not automatically establish synthetic origin.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
