    {
      "id": "ZHCJ",
      "code_hash": "3b6f474b094a34bfafa292404bbf524b",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"4-what-does-the-forest-rely-on\">4. What does the forest rely on?</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The forest has no single logistic-style weight vector. I measure importance\nas the <strong>drop in OOB macro-F1 when a feature is shuffled</strong>. For each tree I\nshuffle only its unsampled compounds, then aggregate class-probability votes.\nHere the target is the same three LD50 bins as above; the older binary\nlow-LD50-versus-rest diagnostic is deliberately not substituted.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Positive drop means the forest relies on the feature. Near-zero or negative\ndrop means little measured reliance or a possible improvement after shuffling.\nTen-repeat SD describes <strong>stability</strong>, not statistical significance or a\nconfidence interval. Correlated features can share importance. OOB permits\nfamiliar scaffolds in training and is not the held-out accuracy headline.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
